#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193826 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{7}{2y + 4} \\\\\nx(2y + 4) &= 7 \\\\\n2xy + 4x &= 7 \\\\\n2xy &= 7 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6512 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
